WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 314: Mary I: Bloody Mary, Philip of Spain, the Smithfield Fires, and the Loss of Calais

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.720
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:13.840
[Rebecca] Today, our Kings and Queens of England series examines the first woman to successfully claim and wear the English crown.

4
00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:19.1000
[Rebecca] A sovereign of unyielding faith whose reign became an inferno of martyrdom and unfulfilled sorrow.

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:27.760
[Rebecca] Queen Mary I hear is George with the tragic, powerful story of Mary Tudor.

6
00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:36.560
[George] Born in February 1516 at Greenwich Palace, Princess Mary was the apple of Henry VIII's eye during her golden childhood.

7
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:43.200
[George] Henry proudly carried the toddler before Venetian ambassadors, boasting, This girl never cries.

8
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:52.160
[George] Educated in Latin and courtly arts by the Spanish humanist Juan Luis Vives, Mary was invested as nominal governor of the Welsh marches at Ludlow.

9
00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:59.200
[George] But when Henry broke with Rome to marry Anne Bolin in 1533, Mary's world was violently shattered.

10
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:08.400
[George] Stripped of her royal title of princess and declared an illegitimate bastard, Mary was forced to serve as a lady in waiting to her infant half-sister Elizabeth.

11
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:16.400
[George] For years, she was forbidden from visiting her dying mother Catherine of Aragon, denied permission even to send a farewell letter.

12
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:29.200
[George] When Henry demanded she sign an oath acknowledging her illegitimacy and the royal supremacy in 1536, Mary resisted with fierce bravery until her mentors warned her that refusal meant death on the scaffold.

13
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:30.400
[George] Under Edward V.

14
00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:44.720
[George] I, Mary defied royal decrees by celebrating traditional Latin Mass in her private chapel, hiding behind her cousin Emperor Charles V's diplomatic protection, forging an unyielding, martyrdom-tempered devotion to the Catholic faith.

15
00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:51.360
[George] Mary's early education under Juan Louis Vives was one of the most rigorous ever provided to a European princess.

16
00:01:51.360 --> 00:02:02.600
[George] She translated Erasmus's paraphrase of the Gospel of John from Latin into English, studied Greek, read Christian poets like Prudentius, and mastered the virginals and lute.

17
00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:06.600
[George] Yet her teenage years were marked by profound psychological trauma.

18
00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:13.720
[George] When her mother was cast aside, Mary was forbidden from seeing her even as Catherine lay dying at Kimbleton Castle.

19
00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:28.280
[George] When Henry VIII forced her to sign the document acknowledging that her mother's marriage was incestuous and that she was a bastard, Mary signed in tears, locking her bitter humiliation away behind a wall of cold, unyielding religious piety.

20
00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:29.400
[George] When Edward V.

21
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:37.960
[George] I died and Northumberland attempted to kidnap Mary to crown Lady Jane Grey, the thirty-seven-year-old princess revealed iron courage.

22
00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:49.560
[George] Riding through the night across the East Anglian Fens to Kenninghall and Framlingham Castle, Mary raised the royal standard, dispatching letters to every town and guild commanding their allegiance.

23
00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:53.480
[George] Her appeal to hereditary legitimacy galvanized the realm.

24
00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:59.400
[George] Thousands of yeomen and aristocrats flocked to her fortress, dwarfing Northumberland's forces.

25
00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:10.440
[George] On August 3, 1553, Mary made her triumphant royal entry into London through Aldgate, riding alongside her glamorous twenty-year-old half-sister Elizabeth.

26
00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:15.960
[George] Crowds cheered hysterically, showering the new queen with rose petals and singing hymns.

27
00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:27.400
[George] Crowned at Westminster Abbey on October 1 in a lavish six-hour service anointed with holy oil blessed by the Bishop of Eris, Mary was the first crowned queen regnant in English history.

28
00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:35.880
[George] She believed her miraculous, bloodless accession was undeniable proof that God had intervened to restore England to the true Catholic fold.

29
00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:51.120
[George] Mary's ride from Hunston to Framlingham Castle in July 1553 was a feat of extraordinary physical endurance, covering over 100 miles in thirty-six hours, the thirty-seven-year-old princess outran Northumberland's cavalry traps.

30
00:03:51.360 --> 00:04:04.640
[George] At Framlingham, she raised the royal standard of England over the medieval curtain walls, welcoming thousands of Norfolk and Suffolen who marched through the summer heat with scythes and longbows to defend their rightful queen.

31
00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:20.000
[George] Her victory was hailed as a miracle, leading Mary to believe with fatal certainty that God had preserved her through twenty years of tribulation for one supreme divine mission to cleanse England of Protestant heresy and restore the realm to the Roman Catholic Church.

32
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:34.720
[George] Determined to secure a Catholic heir and anchor England within the Habsburg Empire, Mary set her heart upon marrying Prince Philip of Spain, the handsome twenty-six-year-old son of Emperor Charles V, despite Philip being eleven years her junior.

33
00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:41.360
[George] The match horrified the English populace and Parliament, who dreaded Spanish Inquisition and foreign wars.

34
00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:48.320
[George] In January 1554, Sir Thomas Wyatt led an armed insurrection of Kentish men marching on London.

35
00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:55.040
[George] As panic swept Whitehall and ministers urged Mary to flee, the Queen rode directly to Guildhall.

36
00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:06.560
[George] Standing before thousands of armed London citizens, Mary delivered an electrifying speech, declaring that she was married to the realm of England and loved her subjects as a mother loves her children.

37
00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:13.280
[George] Her bravery rallied the city, Londoners defended Ludgate Hill, and Wyatt's rebellion was crushed.

38
00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:20.800
[George] In July 1554, Philip landed at Southampton, and the royal wedding was celebrated at Winchester Cathedral.

39
00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:29.920
[George] Though Philip found Mary plain and aging, treating her with polite, cold detachment, Mary fell deeply, obsessively in love with her Spanish prince.

40
00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:47.080
[George] When Mary spoke to the citizens of London at Guildhall during Wyatt's rebellion, she wore her royal coronation ring upon her finger, holding up her hand before the assembled thousands and declaring, I am your queen, to whom at my coronation you promised your faith and allegiance.

41
00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:53.160
[George] When I was married to this realm, I was wedded to you, and I love you as a mother loveth her children.

42
00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:55.320
[George] Her courage electrified the city.

43
00:05:55.320 --> 00:06:01.400
[George] When Wyatt's rebel army reached the gates, Londoners closed the portcullis and fought from the rooftops.

44
00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:13.000
[George] Her marriage to Philip at Winchester Cathedral was celebrated with immense Spanish pomp, but Philip privately wrote to his advisers that he was embarking on a crusade of duty rather than a union of love.

45
00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:22.200
[George] In November 1554, Mary achieved the supreme spiritual objective of her life, England's formal reconciliation with the papacy.

46
00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:31.640
[George] Cardinal Reginald Pohl, the exiled English prelate and papal legate, sailed up the Thames in a magnificent royal barge adorned with a silver cross.

47
00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:42.040
[George] On November 30, in the great chamber of Whitehall Palace, King Philip and Queen Mary knelt before Cardinal Pole alongside the assembled lords and commons of Parliament.

48
00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:52.840
[George] In solemn Latin, Pohl pronounced the papal absolution of the realm, absolving England from the sin of schism and welcoming the nation back into the embrace of the Catholic Church.

49
00:06:52.840 --> 00:07:02.680
[George] Members of Parliament wept, chanting the Tea Diem, while Mary felt a sudden flutter in her womb, joyfully convinced that she was pregnant with a Catholic heir.

50
00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:18.640
[George] Parliament repealed all of Henry VIII's antipapal legislation, restoring papal authority and reviving the ancient medieval heresy statutes, de Heretico Comburendo, setting the legal machinery in motion for the systematic persecution of religious dissenters.

51
00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:29.120
[George] Cardinal Reginald Pohl, the grandson of George, Duke of Clarence, had spent twenty years in Italian exile with a price on his head placed by Henry Vii.

52
00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:33.840
[George] His return to England as papal legate was greeted with weeping joy by the Queen.

53
00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:41.520
[George] When Pole delivered his historic address to Parliament in Whitehall, he declared, I am come to reconcile, not to condemn.

54
00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:46.880
[George] I come not to compel, but to call again, I am come to open the door of the sanctuary.

55
00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:50.480
[George] Yet Pole and Mary fatally misjudged the English mood.

56
00:07:50.480 --> 00:08:06.320
[George] While the aristocracy was willing to accept Catholic ceremonial, they fiercely refused to surrender the vast monastic lands confiscated under Henry Viii, forcing the Pope to confirm the nobility in their ill-gotten church estates before reconciliation could occur.

57
00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:16.080
[George] In February 1555, the execution fires were lit, beginning an era of religious terror that forever earned Mary the moniker, Bloody Mary.

58
00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:28.080
[George] Over the next three years, two hundred and eighty-four Protestant men, women and teenagers were burned alive at the stake across England for refusing to accept the Catholic Mass and transubstantiation.

59
00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:35.840
[George] In October 1555, at Oxford, bishops Hugh Latimer and Nicholas Ridley were tied back to back to a wooden post.

60
00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:43.040
[George] As the faggots were lit, Latimer famously called out, Be of good comfort, Master Ridley, and play the man.

61
00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:49.920
[George] We shall this day light such a candle, by God's grace, in England, as I trust shall never be put out.

62
00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:55.200
[George] Ridley perished in agonizing slowness as the fire smouldered around his legs.

63
00:08:55.200 --> 00:09:06.680
[George] In March 1556, Archbishop Thomas Cranmer, who had recanted six times under psychological pressure in prison, made a sensational final stand at the University Church of St.

64
00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:07.320
[George] Mary.

65
00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:12.520
[George] Recanting his recantations, Cranmer declared his hand had offended in signing them.

66
00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:20.280
[George] And when tied to the stake, he thrust his right hand into the roaring flames first, shouting, This hand hath offended.

67
00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:28.280
[George] Far from suppressing Protestantism, the public burnings horrified ordinary citizens, turning heretics into immortal martyrs.

68
00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:38.120
[George] The Smithfield burnings were conducted under the ancient heresy statutes revived by Parliament, managed with bureaucratic precision by Bishop Edmund Bonner of London.

69
00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:41.640
[George] Yet the cruelty of the executions appalled the populace.

70
00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:49.480
[George] Pregnant women were burned, and when one delivered an infant in the fire at Guernsey, the bailiff threw the baby back into the flames.

71
00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:53.400
[George] Latimer and Ridley's martyrdom at Oxford became legendary.

72
00:09:53.400 --> 00:10:08.440
[George] Latimer died quickly in the flames, but Ridley suffered horrific agony as the wood was green and packed so tightly around him that his lower legs burned away while his upper body was untouched, crying out in torment, Lord, have mercy upon me.

73
00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:11.960
[George] Let the fire come unto me, I cannot burn.

74
00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:17.400
[George] While the fires blazed, Mary's personal life descended into tragic humiliation.

75
00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:25.640
[George] In April 1555, as her expected delivery date approached, the Queen withdrew into seclusion at Hampton Court Palace.

76
00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:29.080
[George] Rumours swept Europe that she had given birth to a boy.

77
00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:32.200
[George] Church bells rang and ambassadors were summoned.

78
00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:35.800
[George] But weeks turned into months, and no baby appeared.

79
00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:47.840
[George] Mary's swollen abdomen was not a child, but a phantom pregnancy, pseudosiaesis, caused by an ovarian tumor or intense hormonal imbalance combined with desperate psychological yearning.

80
00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:53.520
[George] In August, humiliated and broken, Mary emerged from her rooms, childless.

81
00:10:53.520 --> 00:11:04.320
[George] Philip, viewing England as a useless pawn that could not provide him with an heir, departed for Flanders in September 1555, leaving Mary in devastated solitude.

82
00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:10.480
[George] He returned only briefly in 1557 to drag a reluctant England into his war with France.

83
00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:23.120
[George] When a second phantom pregnancy occurred in early 1558, Mary was forced to realize that God had denied her a Catholic heir, leaving the succession inexorably to her Protestant half-sister Elizabeth.

84
00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:28.320
[George] Mary's phantom pregnancy of 1555 was an agonizing national tragedy.

85
00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:40.480
[George] For months, nursery rooms were decorated at Hampton Court, gold-trimmed cradles were prepared, and royal letters announcing the birth of a prince were drafted with blank spaces left for the date and sex.

86
00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:47.280
[George] As the weeks dragged on and Mary's labor failed to materialize, courtiers realized the terrible truth.

87
00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:56.400
[George] Philip could barely conceal his disgust at her hysterical clinging and pathetic demands for affection, quickly finding excuses to depart for Flanders.

88
00:11:56.400 --> 00:12:07.120
[George] Mary's letters to Philip in Brussels were heart-wrenching pleas of unrequited love, writing of her desolate loneliness and begging him to return to an England that detested his very name.

89
00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:12.080
[George] The crowning catastrophe of Mary's reign fell in January 1558.

90
00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:23.280
[George] A surprise French assault led by the Duke of Guise captured Calais, the last continental possession of the English crown, held since Edward III's victory in 1347.

91
00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:27.760
[George] The loss of Calais was an apocalyptic blow to English national pride.

92
00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:32.040
[George] After two centuries, England was entirely expelled from Europe.

93
00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:40.600
[George] Mary was inconsolable, reportedly telling her weeping ladies, When I am dead and opened, you shall find Calais lying in my heart.

94
00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:49.800
[George] Plagued by ovarian cancer and ravaged by influenza during an epidemic that swept southern England, 42-year-old Queen Mary lay dying at St.

95
00:12:49.960 --> 00:12:53.080
[George] James's Palace in November 1558.

96
00:12:53.080 --> 00:13:00.920
[George] On November 17, as Mass was celebrated in her bedchamber and the priest elevated the host, Mary breathed her last.

97
00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:03.800
[George] Cardinal Pole died twelve hours later.

98
00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:13.880
[George] In London, church bells rang not in mourning, but in celebration as heralds proclaimed Queen Elizabeth I buried in Westminster Abbey beneath a simple stone tomb.

99
00:13:13.880 --> 00:13:21.240
[George] Mary was later joined by Elizabeth, where an inscription reads, Partners in throne and grave, here we sleep.

100
00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:25.240
[George] Elizabeth and Mary, sisters, in hope of the resurrection.

101
00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:29.640
[George] The surrender of Calais was a mortal blow to Mary's broken spirit.

102
00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:39.560
[George] For two hundred and eleven years, the English standard had flown over the continental port, its loss left England without a single foot of continental territory.

103
00:13:39.560 --> 00:13:50.440
[George] As influenza raged through London in the autumn of 1558, killing Mary's ministers and over ten percent of the southern English population, the Queen's health collapsed.

104
00:13:50.440 --> 00:14:02.680
[George] When her counselors finally persuaded her to formally name her Protestant half sister Elizabeth as her successor, Mary wept, begging only that Elizabeth maintain the Catholic faith and pay the royal debts.

105
00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:15.000
[George] Her death closed five turbulent years of religious terror, leaving behind a nation traumatized by fire, irrevocably suspicious of Spanish influence, and ready to embrace the Protestant Elizabethan age.

106
00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:20.640
[Rebecca] Thank you for joining us for this episode of Most Fascinating Stories.

107
00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:36.400
[Rebecca] If you enjoyed this chapter of Mary I, Bloody Mary, Philip of Spain, The Smithfield Fires, and the Loss of Calais, subscribe to the show, share it with a fellow history lover, and tune in next Tuesday for our next journey into history's most extraordinary events.

108
00:14:36.400 --> 00:14:38.880
[Rebecca] Until next time, I'm Rebecca.

